## Why This Is Asked
Tests an engineer's ability to design incremental statistics with tight per-batch updates and constant-time queries, a realistic analytics constraint.

## Key Concepts
- Maintaining a nonlinear statistic (entropy) via a linearizable surrogate S
- Precomputing k log k and handling zero edges cleanly
- Incremental updates using two counters per move

## Code Example
```javascript
// G[k] = k * Math.log(k), with G[0] = 0
function processBatch(batch, freq, G, S, n) {
  for (const {from,to} of batch) {
    const fFrom = freq[from];
    freq[from] = fFrom - 1;
    S.value += G[fFrom - 1] - G[fFrom];
    const fTo = freq[to];
    freq[to] = fTo + 1;
    S.value += G[fTo + 1] - G[fTo];
  }
  return Math.log(n) - S.value / n;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt if m changes over time or updates include new categories?
- How do you handle numerical stability for very large n and skewed distributions?

## Why This Question Is Asked
Tests the ability to balance space, accuracy, and latency for streaming distinct counts under a sliding window constraint.

## Key Concepts
- Counting Bloom Filter with aging counters
- Sliding window semantics and estimation bias
- Memory-accuracy trade-offs: probabilistic versus exact data structures

## Code Example
```javascript
class SlidingWindowCBF {
  constructor(m, k, windowSize) {
    this.counters = new Array(m).fill(0);
    this.ringBuffer = new Array(windowSize);
    this.head = 0;
    this.k = k;
  }

  hash(id, j) {
    const h = (id * 31 + j * 17) % m;
    return Math.abs(h);
  }

  add(id) {
    for (let j = 0; j < this.k; j++) {
      this.counters[this.hash(id, j)]++;
    }
    this.ringBuffer[this.head] = id;
    this.head = (this.head + 1) % this.ringBuffer.length;
  }

  remove(id) {
    for (let j = 0; j < this.k; j++) {
      this.counters[this.hash(id, j)]--;
    }
  }

  estimateDistinct() {
    return this.counters.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) / this.k;
  }
}
```

